   "rule_summary": "Each battery model must undergo a sustained-heat test at 55 °C using a regulator-approved protocol and representative samples. The target maximum failure rate is 5% for any model.",
   "comparison": "weakened by: over_limit_consequence, transition",
   "final_rule_text": "Each battery model must undergo a sustained-heat test at 55 °C using a regulator-approved protocol and representative samples. The target maximum failure rate is 5% for any model. Before sale and at least annually thereafter, manufacturers must test and submit per-model sample sizes, methods, exact failure rates, and corrective actions to the responsible regulator. Customers considering or owning a model must receive its exact per-model failure rate, test date and method, and any safety limitations, precautions, or corrective actions in plain language. Individual-company per-model failure rates must not be published to the general public; regulators may publish anonymized or aggregated results. A model exceeding 5% must be promptly reported to the regulator, undergo confirmatory testing and corrective action, and must not be represented as meeting the 5% criterion until it does. The regulator shall promptly assess evidence and risk and may authorize continued or conditional sales during corrective action, with customer disclosures and safeguards, or impose interim sales restrictions where warranted. An exceedance alone does not automatically require sales suspension."
  },
